hadiths[264]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17191","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning caution, decree, and the duty of supplication. > **""Caution will never avail against decree, but supplication avails against what has descended and what has not yet descended; so hold fast to supplication, O servants of Allah.""**"
"17192","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of caution, decree, and supplication meeting affliction. > **""Caution does not avail against decree, but supplication avails against what has descended and what has not yet descended, and supplication meets tribulation and they wrestle together until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17193","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of caution, the decree, and the wrestling of supplication with tribulation. > **""Caution does not avail against decree, and supplication avails so long as the decree has not descended, and tribulation and supplication meet between heaven and earth and wrestle together until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17194","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, رضي الله عنه, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ speak of provision, obedience, and supplication. > **""Provision is neither diminished by disobedience nor increased by good deeds, and abandoning supplication is disobedience.""**"
"17195","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, states as his own saying concerning miserliness with greeting and incapacity in supplication. > **""The most miserly of people is he who is miserly with greeting, and the most helpless of people is he who is helpless in supplication.""**"
"17196","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a people afflicted with trial and questioned them concerning well-being. > **""Did these people not ask Allah for well-being?""**"
"17197","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, رضي الله عنه, narrates that on the day of Badr he fought for a time, then hurried to see what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was doing and found him in prostration repeating two words without adding to them, returning to battle and finding him so each time until Allah granted victory. > **""O Living, O Sustainer, O Living, O Sustainer.""**"
"17198","ʿAlī, رضي الله عنه, also narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the station of supplication for the believer, the religion, and the heavens and earth. > **""Supplication is the weapon of the believer, the pillar of the religion, and the light of the heaven and the earth.""**"
"17199","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to guide them to deliverance from their enemy and increase of provision through constant supplication. > **""Shall I not guide you to what will deliver you from your enemy and increase your provisions for you? Call upon Allah in your night and your day, for supplication is the weapon of the believer.""**"
"17200","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the servant remaining upon good unless he hastens, and when asked how he hastens, he described his words. > **""The servant remains upon good so long as he does not hasten. I have called upon my Lord and He has not answered me.""**"
"17201","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the Muslim supplicating upon the earth, the conditions of sin and severing kinship, and the meaning of hastening, after which a man asked about increasing supplication. > **""There is no Muslim man upon the face of the earth who calls upon Allah with a supplication except that He grants it to him or averts from him an equal evil, so long as he does not call for sin or severing of kinship, and so long as he does not hasten. I have called and called and I have not been answered. Allah is Most Generous in giving.""**"
"17202","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the most excellent worship as expectant waiting for divine relief. > **""The most excellent worship is awaiting relief from Allah.""**"
"17203","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened hearts to vessels, some more retentive than others, and commanded the people to ask with certainty. > **""Hearts are vessels, and some of them are more retentive than others, so when you ask Allah, O people, ask Him while you are certain of the answer, for Allah does not answer a servant who calls upon Him from a heedless, inattentive heart.""**"
"17204","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reported the words of Allah the Exalted concerning His servant's opinion of Him and His companionship in supplication. > **""Allah the Exalted says: I am with My servant's thought of Me, and I am with him when he calls upon Me.""**"
"17205","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened hearts to vessels, the best most retentive, and commanded asking with confidence while warning against the heedless heart. > **""These hearts are vessels, and the best of them are the most retentive, so when you ask Allah, ask Him while you are confident of the answer, for Allah does not answer the supplication of one who calls from a heedless, inattentive heart.""**"
"17206","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, swears by Him besides whom there is no god concerning the servant's good opinion of Allah and where all good lies. > **""By Him besides whom there is no god, no servant thinks well of Allah except that He grants him his expectation, and that is because all good is in His hand.""**"
"17207","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ the words of Allah concerning His servant's opinion of Him. > **""Allah says: I am with My servant's opinion of Me.""**"
"17208","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the Muslim directing his face to Allah in asking and the two forms of divine giving. > **""There is no Muslim who directs his face to Allah in asking except that He grants it to him, either hastening it for him or storing it for him.""**"
"17209","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, is reported with the narration that every Muslim who calls upon Allah is answered in one of two ways, unless he asks for sin or severing kinship. > **""There is no Muslim who calls upon Allah with anything except that He answers him in it, either granting it to him or expiating thereby a sin from him, so long as he does not call for sin or severing of kinship.""**"
"17210","Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of the Muslim supplication free of sin and severing kinship being granted in three ways, and when the people said they would increase, he spoke of Allah's abundance. > **""There is no Muslim who calls with a supplication in which there is no sin or severing of kinship except that Allah grants him thereby one of three: either He hastens his supplication for him, or He stores it for him in the Hereafter, or He averts from him an equal evil. Allah is Most Abundant.""**"
"17211","Salmān, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the address of Allah, Blessed and Exalted, to the son of Ādam dividing three matters between Lord and servant. > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says to the son of Adam: O son of Adam, there are three things, one for Me, one for you, and one between Me and you. As for what is for Me, you worship Me and associate nothing with Me. As for what is for you, whatever deed you do I recompense you for it, and if I forgive then I am the Forgiving, the Merciful. As for what is between Me and you, from you is supplication and asking, and upon Me is answering and giving.""**"
"17212","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah, the Exalted, as modest and generous toward His servant's raised hands. > **""Allah the Exalted is Modest and Generous; He is shy before His servant, when he raises his hands, to return them empty with nothing in them.""**"
"17213","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning Allah's modesty before the grey-haired Muslim who holds straight to the Sunnah when he asks. > **""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is shy before the grey-haired Muslim, when he is upright and constant upon the Sunnah, to have him ask Allah and not grant him.""**"
"17214","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of those freed from the Fire each day and night and the Muslim's answered call. > **""Allah has those freed from the Fire in every day and night, and for every Muslim in every day and night there is an answered supplication.""**"
"17215","Jābir, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of those freed from the Fire each day and night in the month of Ramaḍān and the Muslim's supplication therein. > **""Allah has in every day and night in the month of Ramaḍān those freed from the Fire, and for every Muslim there is a supplication he calls with and is answered for it.""**"
"17216","Ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of four gifts met with four gifts, expounded in the Book of Allah with remembrance, supplication, gratitude, and seeking forgiveness. > **""Whoever is given four is given four, and their exposition is in the Book of Allah the Exalted: whoever is given remembrance, Allah remembers him, for Allah says: Remember Me, I shall remember you. Whoever is given supplication is given the answer, for Allah says: Call upon Me, I shall answer you. Whoever is given gratitude is given increase, for Allah says: If you are grateful, I shall surely increase you. Whoever is given seeking forgiveness is given forgiveness, for Allah says: Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed He is Most Forgiving.""**"
"17217","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked a man of the Arabs to whom they flee in hope and fear, to whom they return when answered, and what they know of the answer, repeating his rebuke thrice. > **""When hope or fear befalls you, to whom do you flee? To whom do you return when He answers you? You know and you do not act; you know and you do not act.""**"
"17218","Abū Mūsā, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ the address of Allah the Exalted to His servants concerning guidance, strength, and richness, the gathering of first and last upon the most pious or most wicked heart not adding or diminishing His dominion, and His power over forgiveness. > **""Allah the Exalted says: O My servants, all of you are astray except whom I guide, and weak except whom I strengthen, and poor except whom I enrich, so ask Me and I shall give you. Had your first and your last, your living and your dead, your moist and your dry, gathered upon the heart of the most pious servant of Mine, it would not increase My dominion by a gnat's wing. And had they gathered upon the heart of the most wicked servant of Mine, they would not diminish My dominion by a gnat's wing. That is because I am One; My punishment is speech and My mercy is speech. Whoever is certain of My power over forgiveness, it is not great in My sight to forgive his sins though they be many.""**"
"17219","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that a man came saying forgive me and Muḥammad and admit none with us into mercy, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked who said it and the man owned it. > **""Who said it? You have veiled it from many people.""**"
"17220","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded abundance in wishing, for the wisher asks his Lord. > **""When one of you wishes, let him wish abundantly, for he is but asking his Lord, the Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"17221","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded each one to ask his Lord for his need and all his needs, even the lace of his sandal and even salt. > **""Let one of you ask his Lord for his need, indeed all his needs, until he asks Him for the strap of his sandal when it breaks, and until he asks Him for salt.""**"
"17222","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, states as her own saying that every thing, even the sandal strap, is to be asked of Allah, for what He does not ease is not eased. > **""Ask Allah for everything, even the sandal strap, for if Allah does not ease it, it is not eased.""**"
"17223","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, states that the most beloved supplication to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was to supplicate three times. > **""The most beloved supplication to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was to supplicate three times.""**"
"17224","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, رضي الله عنهما, narrates from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that when the servant whom Allah loves calls, Allah tells Jibrīl to fulfill but delay his need out of love for his voice, and when the servant whom He dislikes calls, to fulfill and hasten out of dislike for his voice. > **""The servant calls upon Allah, and He loves him, so Allah says: O Jibrīl, fulfill this servant's need but delay it, for I love to hear his voice. And the servant calls upon Allah, and He dislikes him, so Allah says: O Jibrīl, fulfill this servant's need and hasten it, for I dislike to hear his voice.""**"
"17225","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the man who seeks a need and Allah turns it away for what is better, yet he wrongfully accuses people and asks who suffices him. > **""A man seeks a need and Allah turns it away from him for what is better for him, then he accuses people, wronging them, saying: who suffices me?""**"
"17226","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the wisher to consider his wish, for he knows not what of it is decreed for him. > **""When one of you wishes, let him consider what he wishes for, for he does not know what of his wish is written for him.""**"
"17227","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed the answered supplication of the oppressed even if sinful, his sin being against himself. > **""The supplication of the oppressed is answered, even if he be sinful, for his sinfulness is against himself.""**"
"17228","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning three whom Allah has bound Himself not to refuse: the fasting until he breaks, the oppressed until aided, and the traveller until he returns. > **""Three: Allah has bound Himself not to refuse them a supplication: the fasting person until he breaks his fast, the oppressed until he is aided, and the traveller until he returns.""**"
"17229","In a narration from him the wording is that three are not refused, among them the one who remembers Allah, followed by the like of the preceding report. > **""Three whose supplication is not rejected: the one who remembers Allah.""**"
"17230","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning two jealousies, one loved and one hated, two prides, one loved and one hated, and three whose supplication is answered. > **""Two jealousies, one Allah loves and the other Allah hates: jealousy in suspicion Allah loves, and jealousy without suspicion Allah hates. And pride when a man gives charity Allah loves, and pride in arrogance Allah hates. Three whose supplication is answered: the father, the traveller, and the oppressed.""**"
"17231","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of two supplications between which and Allah there is no veil. > **""Two supplications with no veil between them and Allah: the supplication of the oppressed and the supplication of a man for his brother behind his back.""**"
"17232","Khuzaymah ibn Thābit, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned against the supplication of the oppressed, describing its ascent and the oath of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, to aid it even after a while. > **""Fear the supplication of the oppressed, for it is carried upon the clouds; Allah says: By My might and My majesty, I shall surely aid you even if after a while.""**"
"17233","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that while circumambulating with the Prophet ﷺ they heard a man saying forgive so-and-son-of-so, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked his reason, he said a man had charged him to supplicate for him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ gave him glad tidings. > **""What is this? Your companion has been forgiven.""**"
"17234","ʿImrān ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed the supplication of a brother for his brother in his absence. > **""The supplication of a brother for his brother behind his back is not rejected.""**"
"17235","Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Asadī reports that he heard Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrate two counsels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, one concerning the oppressed even if an unbeliever, and one concerning doubt. > **""The supplication of the oppressed, even if he be an unbeliever, has no veil before it. Leave what puts you in doubt for what does not put you in doubt.""**"
"17236","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the angels' response when a man supplicates for his brother in his absence. > **""When a man supplicates for his brother in his absence, the angels say: Amen, and the like for you.""**"
"17237","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after the salutation with his face toward the qiblah, raised his head and supplicated for Salamah ibn Hishām, ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd, and the weak Muslims who can contrive no plan and find no way. > **""O Allah, deliver Salamah ibn Hishām, ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd, and the weak Muslims who can contrive no plan and find no way.""**"
"17238","Abū Ayyūb, رضي الله عنه, narrates as an observed practice of the Prophet ﷺ that whenever he supplicated, he began with himself, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17239","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, narrates that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which supplication is most excellent, and he answered her with a single sentence. > **""The supplication of a man for himself.""**"
"17240","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning a man whose rank is raised and who asks whence it came, and is told of his child's supplication. > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, raises a rank for a man, and he says: Whence is this for me? And He says: By your child's supplication for you.""**"
"17241","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, رضي الله عنه, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ curse the one who asks by the Face of Allah and the one asked by it who then refuses his asker, unless the request be for severance. > **""Cursed is he who asks by the Face of Allah, and cursed is he who is asked by the Face of Allah and then refuses his asker, unless he asks for severance.""**"
"17242","A man reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, instructed a man concerning the manner of asking his Lord for good. > **""When you ask your Lord for good, do not ask with a stone in your hand.""**"
"17243","Ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the descent of Allah in the remaining third of the night, the opening of heaven's gates, His outstretched hand, and His call to askers until dawn. > **""When the remaining third of the night comes, Allah descends to the lowest heaven, then the gates of heaven are opened, then He extends His hand and says: Is there an asker who shall be given his asking? And it remains so until dawn breaks.""**"
"17244","ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of a crier calling every night for supplicants, askers, and seekers of forgiveness until dawn. > **""A crier calls every night: Is there a caller that he be answered? Is there an asker that he be given? Is there a seeker of forgiveness that he be forgiven? Until dawn breaks.""**"
"17245","ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ al-Thaqafī, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the opening of heaven's gates at midnight, the crier's call to callers, askers, and the distressed, and the two exceptions. > **""The gates of heaven are opened at midnight, and a crier calls: Is there a caller that he be answered? Is there an asker that he be given? Is there a distressed one that he be relieved? And no Muslim calls with a supplication except that Allah answers him, except an adulteress who earns by her shame or a collector of tolls.""**"
"17246","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the nightly descent of Allah to the lowest heaven and His call until dawn. > **""Allah descends every night to the lowest heaven and says: Is there an asker that I give him? Is there a seeker of forgiveness that I forgive him? Until dawn rises.""**"
"17247","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, رضي الله عنه, narrates in the manner of the report of Abū Hurayrah, with the wording that but for hardship upon his nation he would command the toothstick at every prayer and delay the night prayer to the first third, for when the first third passes Allah descends and remains until dawn while a caller proclaims the invitations to askers, callers, the sick, and sinners. > **""Had I not feared hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them with the toothstick at every prayer, and I would have delayed the night prayer to the first third of the night, for when the first third of the night passes, Allah descends to the lowest heaven and remains there until dawn, and a caller says: Is there an asker who shall be given? Is there a caller who shall be answered? Is there a sick one seeking cure who shall be cured? Is there a sinner seeking forgiveness who shall be forgiven?""**"
"17248","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the descent of Allah when a third of the night remains and His successive calls to callers, forgivers, providers, and sufferers until dawn. > **""When a third of the night remains, Allah descends to the lowest heaven and says: Who is it that calls upon Me that I answer him? Who is it that seeks My forgiveness that I forgive him? Who is it that seeks provision of Me that I provide him? Who is it that asks Me to uncover harm that I uncover it from him? Until dawn rises.""**"
"17249","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the descent of Allah in the last half of the night, or a third, with His calls to callers, askers, and forgivers until dawn and the worshipper's departure from dawn prayer. > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, descends to the heaven of the world in the last half of the night, or a third, and says: Who is it that calls upon Me that I answer him? Who is it that asks of Me that I give him? Who is it that seeks My forgiveness that I forgive him? Until dawn rises and the reciter departs from the dawn prayer.""**"
"17250","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the descent of our Lord when a third of the night remains and His successive calls to His servants, the wrongdoer, the straitened, the oppressed, and the bound, until morning. > **""Our Lord, Blessed and Exalted, descends to the lowest heaven when a third of the night remains and says: Is there no servant of Mine who calls upon Me that I answer him? Is there no wrongdoer against himself who calls upon Me that I forgive him? Is there no straitened one that I provide him? Is there no oppressed one who calls upon Me that I aid him? Is there no bound one that I free him? And it remains so until the morning brightens.""**"
"17251","Abū al-Dardāʾ, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the descent of Allah in the last three remaining hours of the night: beholding in the first the Book none else beholds, effacing and confirming, beholding in the second the Garden of ʿAdn with prophets, martyrs, and the truthful, then descending in the last hour with calls to forgivers, askers, and callers, for which reason the dawn recital is witnessed by Allah and the angels. > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, descends in the last three hours remaining of the night, and beholds in the first hour the Book which none but He beholds, effacing what He wills and confirming, and beholds in the second hour the Garden of ʿAdn, His dwelling wherein none shall be with Him but prophets, martyrs, and the truthful, wherein is what no eye has seen nor has occurred to a human heart, then descends in the last hour of the night and says: Is there a seeker of forgiveness who seeks My forgiveness that I forgive him? Is there an asker who asks of Me that I give him? Is there a caller who calls upon Me? And for that Allah says: And the recital of dawn; indeed the recital of dawn is witnessed. So Allah and the angels witness it.""**"
"17252","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that a man called out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking which part of the night best answers supplication, and he answered with two words. > **""The last hollow of the night.""**"
"17253","Abū Umāmah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning four stations in which heaven's gates are opened and supplication is answered: the meeting of ranks in Allah's path, rainfall, the establishment of prayer, and the sight of the Kaʿbah. > **""The gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered in four stations: at the meeting of the ranks in the path of Allah, at the descent of rain, at the establishment of prayer, and at the sight of the Kaʿbah.""**"
"17254","Muḥārib ibn Dithār reports from his uncle that he would pass the house of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, at dawn and hear him supplicate, and when he told him of the words, Ibn Masʿūd recalled that Yaʿqūb deferred his sons to the dawn hour. > **""O Allah, You called me and I answered, and You commanded me and I obeyed, and this is the dawn hour, so forgive me.""**"
"17255","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, instructs that whoever of you wishes to ask should begin with praise and extolling of Allah as He deserves, then bless the Prophet ﷺ, then ask after that, for it is worthier of success. > **""When one of you wishes to ask, let him begin with praise and extolling of Allah as He deserves, then let him bless the Prophet ﷺ, then let him ask after that, for it is worthier of success.""**"
"17256","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade making him like the rider's cup, used only at need then set aside or emptied, and commanded his remembrance in supplication. > **""Do not make me like the rider's cup, for the rider fills his cup, and when he is done he hangs up its hangings; if he needs drink or ablution he uses it, otherwise he empties the cup. Remember me at the beginning of supplication, in its middle, and at its end.""**"
"17257","Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd, رضي الله عنه, narrates that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat, a man entered, prayed, then said forgive me and have mercy on me, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him he hastened and taught the order of praise, blessings, then supplication; then another prayed, praised Allah and blessed the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ told him to ask and be given. > **""You hastened, O worshipper. When you pray and sit, praise Allah as He deserves, then bless me, then call upon Him. Ask and you shall be given.""**"
"17258","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ passed Abū ʿAyyāsh Zayd ibn al-Ṣāmit al-Zuraqī while he prayed, saying words of praise and majesty, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared he had called upon Allah by His Greatest Name. > **""O Allah, I ask You by that all praise belongs to You; there is no god but You, O Bestower, O Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour. He has called upon Allah by His Greatest Name, by which when He is called He answers, and when He is asked thereby He gives.""**"
"17259","Abū Ṭalḥah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon a man saying words of praise and majesty, and declared he had asked Allah by the Name that is answered. > **""O Allah, I ask You by that all praise belongs to You; there is no god but You, the Bestower, O Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour. He has asked Allah by His Name by which when He is called He answers.""**"
"17260","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ al-Aslamī, رضي الله عنه, reports that he never heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ call with a supplication except that he opened it with glorification of his Exalted Bestowing Lord. > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Exalted, the Most High, the Bestower.""**"
"17261","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether any supplication is never rejected, and he taught him words of asking by the Name. > **""Yes. Say: I ask You by Your Name, the Highest, the Mightiest, the Most Majestic, the Most Generous.""**"
"17262","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ that he visited ʿĀʾishah one morning and she asked, by his father and mother, to be taught the Name of Allah by which He answers and gives, but he turned his face away; so she rose, performed ablution, and supplicated for all good known and unknown and by His Great Name, whereupon he swore it lies within those names. > **""O Allah, I ask You for all good, what I know of it and what I do not know, and by Your Great Name by which when You are called You answer, and when You are asked thereby You give. By Allah, it is indeed among these names.""**"
"17263","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ that the Greatest Name of Allah, by which when He is called He answers, lies in the verse of Āl ʿImrān through its end. > **""The Greatest Name of Allah, by which when He is called He answers, is in this verse of Āl ʿImrān: Say: O Allah, Master of dominion, You grant dominion to whom You will, to the end of the verse.""**"
"17264","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, رضي الله عنه, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that whoever calls with these five words shall not ask Allah for anything except He grants it. > **""There is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest; there is no god but Allah, alone without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He has power over all things; there is no god but Allah, and there is no might nor power except by Allah.""**"
"17265","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on Friday leading the people in the afternoon prayer, seated in the first two units, when a dog came to cut across his prayer and he feared it would pass; Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, رضي الله عنه, supplicated against the dog and Allah destroyed it by His power; when the Prophet ﷺ finished and saw the dead dog, he asked twice who had called against it until Saʿd owned it, describing his fear for the Prophet's prayer, and when asked how he called, Saʿd repeated his words of glorification and majesty, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ gave him glad tidings of the answered hour. > **""Who among you called against this dog? How did you call against him, O Saʿd? Glory be to You; there is no god but You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour; destroy this dog before it cuts across Your Prophet's prayer. O Saʿd, you have called in a day and an hour with words by which had you called against what is between the heavens and the earth, you would have been answered; so rejoice, O Saʿd.""**"
"17266","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning what to say when seeking a need and hoping for its success, comprising declarations of divine oneness, majesty, and mercy, two Qurʾānic verses, and a comprehensive supplication for mercy, forgiveness, and the fulfillment of needs of this world and the Hereafter. > **""There is no god but Allah, alone without partner, the Exalted, the Tremendous; there is no god but Allah, alone without partner, the Wise, the Generous. In the Name of Allah besides whom there is no god, the Living, the Wise. Glory be to Allah, Lord of the Tremendous Throne. Praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds. As though on the day they see what they are promised they had tarried but an hour of the day; a proclamation, and none shall perish but the rebellious people. As though on the day they see it they had tarried but an evening or its morning. O Allah, I ask You for the causes of Your mercy, the resolves of Your forgiveness, gain from every righteousness, and safety from every sin. O Allah, leave no sin of ours except You forgive it, no anxiety except You relieve it, no debt except You settle it, and no need among the needs of this world and the Hereafter except You fulfill it by Your mercy, O Most Merciful of the merciful.""**"
"17267","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a Bedouin supplicating in his prayer with exalted praise of Him whom eyes do not see and happenings do not change, and a plea for the best of life, deeds, and days; so the Prophet ﷺ set a man over him, summoned him after prayer, gifted him gold brought from a mine, asked his tribe, and when the Bedouin supposed it was for kinship, explained it was for his beautiful extolling of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. > **""O He whom eyes do not see, whom conjectures do not mingle with, whom describers cannot describe, whom happenings do not change, who fears no turns of fortune; who knows the weights of mountains, the measures of seas, the number of raindrops, the number of leaves of trees, the number of what night darkens and day brightens, and what is hidden of heaven from heaven, earth from earth, sea of what is in its depth, and mountain of what is in its ruggedness: make the best of my life its end, the best of my deeds their seal, and the best of my days the day I meet You therein. When he prays, bring him to me. From whom are you, O Bedouin? Do you know why I gifted you the gold? Kinship has a right, but I gifted you the gold for your beautiful praise of Allah.""**"
"17268","Rabīʿah ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbbād, رضي الله عنه, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ command persistence in the address of Majesty and Honour. > **""Persist in: O Possessor of Majesty and Honour.""**"
"17269","Al-Sarī reports from Yaḥyā, from a man of Ṭayy whom he praised highly, that the man would ask Allah to show him the Name by which when He is called He answers, until he saw it inscribed in a star in the sky. > **""O Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour.""**"
"17270","Furāt ibn Salmān reports that ʿAlī, رضي الله عنه, asked whether none of them would rise, pray four units, and say therein what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say, comprising perfect light, mighty forbearance, outstretched giving, noblest Countenance, answering of the compelled, healing, forgiveness, and praise no speaker attains. > **""Your light is perfected and You guided, so praise belongs to You; Your forbearance is mighty and You pardoned, so praise belongs to You; You extended Your hand and gave, so praise belongs to You. Our Lord, Your Face is the noblest of faces, Your station the mightiest of stations, Your gift the best and most wholesome of gifts. Our Lord, You are obeyed and You give thanks; You are disobeyed, Our Lord, and You forgive. You answer the compelled, uncover harm, heal sickness, forgive sin, accept repentance; none can requite Your favours, and no speaker's words can reach Your praise.""**"
"17271","Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, رضي الله عنه, narrates that he passed ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, رضي الله عنه, in the mosque and greeted him, but ʿUthmān did not return it; so he complained to ʿUmar, رضي الله عنه, who summoned ʿUthmān, and ʿUthmān first denied then remembered and sought forgiveness, explaining he had been absorbed in a word heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that veils sight and heart whenever remembered; whereupon Saʿd told him of the first supplication the Prophet ﷺ had mentioned before a Bedouin interrupted him, which Saʿd then pursued and learned. > **""Who is this? Is it Abū Isḥāq? What is it? Yes. The supplication of Dhū al-Nūn when he was in the belly of the fish: There is no god but You; glory be to You; indeed I have been of the wrongdoers. For no Muslim ever calls upon his Lord thereby concerning anything at all except He answers him.""**"
"17272","ʿUmar, meaning Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, رضي الله عنه, narrates that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to call upon Allah to admit her into Paradise, whereupon he magnified the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, describing His Throne and its creaking under His weight. > **""His Throne encompasses the heavens and the earth, and it creaks with a creak like the new saddle when ridden, from His weight.""**"
"17273","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of the servant repeating his Lord's name four times and the answer of Allah, Blessed and Exalted. > **""When the servant says: O Lord, four times, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: At your service, My servant; ask and you shall be given.""**"
"17274","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ hosted a guest, sought food from his wives and found nothing with any of them, then supplicated for bounty and mercy possessed by none but Allah; whereupon a roasted sheep was gifted to him, and he declared it from Allah's bounty while awaiting mercy. > **""O Allah, I ask You for Your bounty and Your mercy, for none possesses them but You. This is from the bounty of Allah, and we await mercy.""**"
"17275","Ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, is reported to have habitually said words addressing the Beginner, the Everlasting, the Living Giver of life to the dead, and the Keeper over every soul for what it earned. > **""O Beginner with no beginning for You, O Everlasting with no passing for You, O Living, Giver of life to the dead, You are the Keeper over every soul for what it earned.""**"
"17276","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, رضي الله عنه, narrates that Abū Bakr, رضي الله عنه, said to rise and seek the aid of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against a hypocrite, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ corrected him concerning from whom aid is sought. > **""Aid is not sought through me; aid is sought through Allah alone, the Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"17277","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ concerning a caller in the Fire crying out with two Names of mercy. > **""A caller in the Fire shall call: O Most Tender, O Bestower.""**"
"17278","ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib, رضي الله عنه, states that every supplication remains veiled until blessings are sent upon Muḥammad ﷺ and the household of Muḥammad. > **""Every supplication is veiled until blessings are sent upon Muḥammad and the household of Muḥammad.""**"
"17279","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, رضي الله عنه, narrates that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what if he made all his prayer for him, and the Prophet ﷺ promised sufficiency for world and Hereafter. > **""If you make all your prayer for me, then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, shall suffice you what concerns you of your world and your Hereafter.""**"
"17280","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposing to devote half, then a third, then all of his prayer as supplication for him, and the Prophet ﷺ permitted each and promised sufficiency for world and Hereafter for the whole. > **""As you wish. Yes. Then it shall suffice you the concern of this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"17281","Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥibbān reports from his father, from his grandfather, that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about devoting a third, then two thirds, then all of his prayer upon him, and the Prophet ﷺ permitted each and promised sufficiency for worldly and otherworldly concerns. > **""Yes, if you wish. Yes. Then Allah shall suffice you what concerns you of your worldly and otherworldly affair.""**"
"17282","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten recorded good deeds for a single blessing upon him. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah writes for him thereby ten good deeds.""**"
"17283","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, رضي الله عنهما, states that whoever sends a single blessing upon the Prophet ﷺ receives seventy prayers from Allah and His angels. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon the Prophet ﷺ, Allah and His angels send seventy prayers upon him.""**"
"17284","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, رضي الله عنه, narrates that he stood in the courtyard of the mosque and saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leave by the gate toward the graveyard; so he waited, followed him into a walled garden of the Aswāf, where the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution, prayed two units, then prostrated a long prostration that made him fear Allah had taken him; and when the Prophet ﷺ finished and he showed himself, the Prophet ﷺ told him of Jibrīl's glad tidings. > **""Jibrīl gave me glad tidings: whoever sends blessings upon me, Allah sends mercy upon him, and whoever sends peace upon me, Allah sends peace upon him.""**"
"17285","In a narration from him, some four or five of the Companions would not leave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his needs by night and day; the narrator followed him into a garden wall of the Aswāf where he prayed and lengthened prostration until the narrator thought his soul taken; then he raised his head, called him, asked his concern, and explained his prostration of thanks for what he was granted concerning his nation. > **""What is with you? I prostrated to my Lord in thanks for what He granted me concerning my nation: whoever of my nation sends one blessing upon me, Allah writes for him ten good deeds and effaces from him ten evil deeds.""**"
"17286","Abū Ṭalḥah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with his face beaming beyond any day seen, asked his state, and the Prophet ﷺ explained that Jibrīl had just left him with tidings of ten good deeds, ten effaced sins, ten raised degrees, and an angel's echo, with an angel entrusted to him from creation until resurrection. > **""Why should my soul not be content and my cheer not show, when Jibrīl but now left me, saying: O Muḥammad, whoever of your nation sends one blessing upon you, Allah writes for him thereby ten good deeds, effaces from him ten evil deeds, raises him thereby ten degrees, and the angel says to him the like of what he said to you. And Allah has entrusted to you an angel from when He created you until He resurrects you; none of your nation sends blessings upon you except he says: And upon you may Allah send mercy.""**"
"17287","In a narration the addition is that Allah returns the like of his words and it shall be presented to him on the Day of Resurrection, and no spoken wording of the addition beyond this report is transmitted. > **""And Allah returns to him the like of his words, and it shall be presented to him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"17288","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out for his need followed only by ʿUmar with a vessel of water; ʿUmar found him prostrating, stepped aside until he raised his head, and the Prophet ﷺ praised his stepping aside and told him of Jibrīl's tidings of tenfold mercy and raised degrees for each blessing. > **""You did well, O ʿUmar, when you stepped aside from me. Jibrīl came to me and said: Whoever sends one blessing upon you, Allah sends ten upon him and raises for him ten degrees.""**"
"17289","ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten prayers from Allah for each blessing sent of one's own accord. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me of his own accord, Allah sends ten upon him thereby.""**"
"17290","Abū Burdah ibn Niyār, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten prayers, ten effaced sins, and ten raised degrees for a blessing sent of one's own accord, with the wording of sincere heart and ten recorded goods in the narration of al-Ṭabarānī. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me of his own accord, Allah sends ten prayers upon him thereby, removes from him ten evil deeds, and raises for him ten degrees.""**"
"17291","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of an angel entrusted to his grave, granted the hearing of creation, who conveys every blessing until the Resurrection with the sender's name and his father's name. > **""Allah has entrusted to my grave an angel granted the hearing of creation, so none sends blessings upon me until the Day of Resurrection except he conveys to me with his name and his father's name: This is so-and-son-of-so; he has sent blessings upon you.""**"
"17292","Ibn al-Ḥimyarī reports that ʿAmmār, رضي الله عنه, offered to narrate from his beloved ﷺ, and when bidden, conveyed the address to ʿAmmār concerning an angel granted all the names of creation, standing at his grave from death until Resurrection, naming each sender and his father while the Lord sends tenfold mercy for each one. > **""O ʿAmmār, Allah has an angel granted all the names of creation, standing at my grave when I die until the Day of Resurrection; none of my nation sends a blessing upon me except he names him with his name and his father's name, saying: O Muḥammad, so-and-so has sent blessings upon you. Then the Lord sends upon that man tenfold for each one.""**"
"17293","In a narration the addition is that the Prophet ﷺ asked his Lord that no servant send a blessing upon him except Allah send tenfold its like upon him, and no spoken wording beyond this report is transmitted. > **""And I asked my Lord that no servant send a blessing upon me except Allah send upon him tenfold its like.""**"
"17294","Abū Umāmah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten mercies from Allah for a single blessing, conveyed by an entrusted angel. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him; an angel is entrusted therewith until he conveys it to me.""**"
"17295","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded blessings wherever they be, for their blessings reach him. > **""Wherever you are, send blessings upon me, for your blessings reach me.""**"
"17296","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of Allah returning his soul for every greeting so that he answers its sender. > **""There is none who sends peace upon me except that Allah returns my soul to me so that I answer him.""**"
"17297","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that a single blessing reaches him, draws his tenfold invocation, and brings ten recorded goods besides. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, his blessing reaches me, I send blessings upon him, and ten good deeds besides are written for him.""**"
"17298","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ graded one, ten, and a hundred blessings with tenfold mercy, a hundredfold mercy, and inscribed freedom from hypocrisy and the Fire with lodging among the martyrs on the Last Day. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him; whoever sends ten upon me, Allah sends a hundred upon him; and whoever sends a hundred upon me, Allah writes for him freedom from hypocrisy between his eyes, freedom from the Fire, and Allah shall lodge him on the Day of Resurrection with the martyrs.""**"
"17299","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised tenfold invocation for a single blessing, with the wording of Allah sending mercy in the narration of al-Nasāʾī. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, I send ten upon him.""**"
"17300","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded blessings from whoever hears his mention. > **""Whoever is mentioned before him, let him send blessings upon me.""**"
"17301","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised ten mercies from Allah for a single blessing upon him. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him.""**"
"17302","Abū Mūsā, رضي الله عنه, narrates from the Prophet ﷺ the promise of ten mercies from Allah for a single blessing upon him. > **""Whoever sends one blessing upon me, Allah sends ten upon him thereby.""**"
"17303","Buraydah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that they said they knew how to greet the Prophet ﷺ and asked how to bless him, whereupon he taught them words placing prayers, mercy, and blessings upon Muḥammad and his household as upon the household of Ibrāhīm. > **""Say: O Allah, place Your prayers, Your mercy, and Your blessings upon Muḥammad and the household of Muḥammad, as You placed them upon the household of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.""**"
"17304","Ruwayfiʿ ibn Thābit, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever blesses Muḥammad and asks for him the Near Station with Allah on the Resurrection shall have his intercession made due. > **""Whoever blesses Muḥammad and says: O Allah, settle him in the Near Station with You on the Day of Resurrection, my intercession becomes due for him.""**"
"17305","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the one who says may Allah reward Muḥammad on our behalf as he deserves, wearing out seventy scribes for a thousand mornings. > **""Whoever says: May Allah reward Muḥammad on our behalf as he deserves, he wears out seventy scribes for a thousand mornings.""**"
"17306","Salāmah al-Kindī reports that ʿAlī, رضي الله عنه, would teach the people blessings upon the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, comprising the Spreader of expanses, Moulder of uplifted heavens, Subduer of hearts upon their nature, the seal, the opener, the aider of truth, the bearer of command without faltering, the guide by whom hearts found guidance, the trusted witness, and the plea for spacious room, multiplied good, completed light, and accepted weighty speech. > **""O Allah, Spreader of expanses, Maker of uplifted heavens, Subduer of hearts upon their nature, wretched and felicitous thereof: place the noblest of Your prayers, the choicest of Your blessings, and the tenderness of Your greeting upon Muḥammad, Your servant and Your Messenger, the seal of what preceded, the opener of what was closed, the aider of truth by truth, the crusher of hosts of falsehood. As he bore he shouldered Your command in obedience to You, hastening toward Your pleasure without turning of foot nor feebleness of resolve, calling to Your revelation, guarding Your covenant, proceeding upon the execution of Your command, until he kindled a brand for the seeker of light, by whom hearts were guided after plunging into trials and sin, with clarifying banners, illuminating lights of Islām, and shining judgments. He is Your trusted trustee, keeper of Your guarded knowledge, Your witness on the Day of Recompense; Your sending him is a blessing, and Your messenger with truth a mercy. O Allah, make spacious for him a spacious place in Your justice, recompense him with multiplied good from Your bounty, congratulated and untroubled, from the triumph of Your dispensed reward and the abundance of Your loosened gift. O Allah, raise his edifice above the edifices of men, honour his resting and lodging with You, complete his light for him, and recompense him for Your sending him with accepted testimony, pleasing speech, truthful utterance, decisive word, proof, and mighty evidence.""**"
"17307","Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that whoever is mentioned before him and misses blessings upon him misses the path to Paradise. > **""Whoever is mentioned before him and misses blessings upon me misses the path to Paradise.""**"
"17308","Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the miser as he before whom he is mentioned yet sends no blessings upon him. > **""The miser is he before whom I am mentioned and he does not send blessings upon me.""**"
"17309","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, and when asked after descending, explained that Jibrīl had come invoking distance for the unforgiven of Ramaḍān, the undutiful to parents, and the one who hears his mention yet withholds blessings, bidding him say amen to each. > **""May the nose of the one who reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven be rubbed in dust; say: Amen. May the nose of the one who reaches his parents and they do not admit him into Paradise be rubbed in dust; may Allah distance him; say: Amen. And a man before whom you are mentioned and he does not send blessings upon you; may Allah distance him; say: Amen.""**"
"17310","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, after which the narrator mentioned the full report, and no spoken wording beyond this frame is transmitted in this report."
"17311","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, then bade the people say amen to distance for whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven, whoever reaches his parents or one of them unforgiven, and whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings. > **""Whoever reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him; say: Amen. Whoever reaches his parents or one of them and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him; say: Amen. And whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon me, may Allah distance him; say: Amen.""**"
"17312","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that while the Prophet ﷺ was upon the pulpit he said amen three times, and when asked explained that Jibrīl had come bidding him say amen to distance for whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings, whoever reaches his parents or one of them and dies unforgiven, and whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven. > **""Jibrīl came to me and said: Whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon you, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen. And whoever reaches his parents or one of them and dies and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen. And whoever reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17313","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit, said amen three times, and when the people asked whether they heard aright, explained that Jibrīl had met him at each step invoking distance and crushing for whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings, whoever reaches his parents or one of them yet is undutiful and enters the Fire, and whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven and enters the Fire. > **""Do you know why I said amen? Jibrīl came to me and said: Whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon you enters the Fire; may Allah distance him and crush him, so I said: Amen. And whoever reaches his parents or one of them and is not dutiful to them enters the Fire; may Allah distance him and crush him, so I said: Amen. And whoever reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven enters the Fire; may Allah distance him and crush him, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17314","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ al-Zubaydī, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque, ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, and when the people remarked upon the new act, explained that Jibrīl had appeared to him at the first step concerning parents, at the second concerning Ramaḍān, and at the third concerning blessings upon mention, invoking doubled distance for each. > **""Jibrīl appeared to me at the first step and said: O Muḥammad, whoever reaches his parents and they do not admit him into Paradise, may Allah distance him, then distance him, so I said: Amen. Then he said to me at the second step: Whoever reaches the month of Ramaḍān and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him, then distance him, so I said: Amen. Then he appeared to me at the third step and said: Whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon you, may Allah distance him, then distance him, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17315","Jābir ibn Samurah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, and when asked after descending, explained that Jibrīl had invoked dust upon the nose of whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven, whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings, and whoever reaches his parents or one of them unforgiven. > **""May the nose of the one who reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven be rubbed in dust; say: Amen. May the nose of the one before whom you are mentioned and he does not send blessings upon you be rubbed in dust; say: Amen. May the nose of the one who reaches his parents or one of them and is not forgiven be rubbed in dust; say: Amen.""**"
"17316","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Prophet ﷺ climbed a step of the pulpit saying amen, then another saying amen, then a third saying amen, then sat; and when asked for what he said amen, explained that Jibrīl had invoked dust upon the nose of whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings, whoever reaches one or both parents yet enters not Paradise, and whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven. > **""Jibrīl came to me and said: May the nose of the one before whom you are mentioned and he does not send blessings upon you be rubbed in dust, so I said: Amen. May the nose of the one who reaches one of his parents or both and does not enter Paradise be rubbed in dust, so I said: Amen. May the nose of the one who reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven be rubbed in dust, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17317","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to the pulpit, saying amen at each of three steps, and when the people remarked upon the unheard speech, asked whether they heard, then explained that Jibrīl had met him at the first step concerning aged parents, at the second concerning withheld blessings, and at the third concerning Ramaḍān, invoking distance for each. > **""Did you hear it? Jibrīl met me when I ascended a step and said: Far removed is whoever reaches his parents in old age or one of them and does not enter Paradise, so I said: Amen. And he said: Far removed is whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon you, so I said: Amen. Then he said: Far removed is whoever reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17318","Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ climbed the steps of the pulpit saying amen at each, then explained that Jibrīl had come saying O Muḥammad, invoking distance for whoever reaches his parents or one of them then enters the Fire, whoever reaches Ramaḍān unforgiven, and whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings. > **""Jibrīl came to me and said: O Muḥammad, whoever reaches his parents or one of them then enters the Fire, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen. Whoever reaches Ramaḍān and is not forgiven, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen. And whoever is mentioned before him and does not send blessings upon you, may Allah distance him; say: Amen, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17319","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit saying amen thrice, and when asked about the new act, explained that Jibrīl had invoked dust or distance for whoever enters Ramaḍān unforgiven, whoever reaches his parents or one of them yet enters not Paradise, and whoever hears his mention yet withholds blessings. > **""May the nose of whoever enters Ramaḍān then is not forgiven be rubbed in dust. Then may the nose of the servant who reaches his parents or one of them then does not enter Paradise be rubbed in dust, or: far removed. Then he said: May the nose of the servant or man before whom you are mentioned and he does not send blessings upon you be rubbed in dust, or: far removed, so I said: Amen.""**"
"17320","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, states as his own saying that blessings from any upon any are not fitting except upon the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Blessings from any upon any are not fitting except upon the Prophet ﷺ.""**"
"17321","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of a man earning lawful wealth and feeding himself, and another with wealth containing charity, who then says words blessing Muḥammad, his servant and messenger, and the believing and submitting men and women, and it becomes purification for him. > **""Sometimes a man earns wealth lawfully and feeds himself, and a man has wealth in which is charity, and he says: O Allah, bless Muḥammad, Your servant and Your messenger, and bless the believing men and believing women, and the submitting men and submitting women; for it is purification for him.""**"
"17322","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that people shall desist from raising their eyes to heaven during supplication lest they be snatched, meaning their sight seized. > **""People shall desist from raising their eyes to heaven during supplication, lest they be snatched.""**"
"17323","Sahl ibn Saʿd, رضي الله عنه, reports that he never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raising his hands aloft when calling upon the pulpit or elsewhere; rather when he called he placed his hands at his shoulders and pointed with his fingers, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17324","Anas, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed Saʿd supplicating with two fingers and commanded him to one. > **""Make it one, O Saʿd.""**"
"17325","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man calling with both fingers together, forbade him, and commanded him to call with one, the right. > **""Call with one of them, with the right.""**"
"17326","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is reported to have looked at a man pointing with two fingers and commanded him twice to one, and no further spoken wording is transmitted in this report. > **""Make it one, make it one.""**"
"17327","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, is reported to have seen a man pointing with two fingers, whereupon he closed one of his fingers and reminded him of divine oneness, and no Prophetic speech is transmitted in this report. > **""Allah is but One God.""**"
"17328","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, states that their raising hands is an innovation, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ added nothing beyond this, meaning the chest, and no Prophetic speech is transmitted in this report."
"17329","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at ʿArafah calling in a described manner: he raised his hands, set them at his thundwah, and turned the bellies of his palms toward the earth, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17330","In a narration the description is that he turned the backs of his palms toward his face, raised them above his thundwah and below his shoulders, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17331","In a narration the description is that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would call at ʿArafah in this manner, meaning with the backs of his palms, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17332","In a narration ʿUthmān describes the raising of Ḥammād, with hands and palms toward the earth, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17333","Khallād ibn al-Sāʾib al-Anṣārī, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when asking, would turn the bellies of his palms toward himself, and when seeking refuge, their backs toward himself, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17334","Ibn ʿAbbās, رضي الله عنهما, reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ calling at ʿArafah with his hands toward his chest, like a beggar seeking food, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17335","ʿĀʾishah, رضي الله عنها, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would raise his hands in calling until she would feel weary for him from how high he raised them, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17336","Abū Barzah al-Aslamī, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands in supplication until the whiteness of his armpits was seen, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17337","Abū Hurayrah, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would raise his hands in supplication until the whiteness of his armpits was seen, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17338","Anas ibn Mālik, رضي الله عنه, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands at ʿArafah in calling, and the Companions said this is earnest entreaty; then the she-camel cried out, so he opened one hand, seized her, while raising the other; in the wording of al-Ṭabarānī the halter fell, he took it and raised his hands, with the addition that this is earnest entreaty and humble weeping, and no Prophetic speech is transmitted in this report."
"17339","Yazīd ibn ʿĀmir, رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ advanced with a party until he halted at Qarn before Murayṭā, raising his hands, facing the qiblah, calling, and no spoken wording is transmitted in this report."
"17340","Ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described their modest Generous Lord who is shy to return uplifted hands empty without good, and taught that whoever raises his hands should say words of the Living, the Sustainer, thrice, then pour the good upon his face when lowering them. > **""Your Lord is Modest and Generous; He is shy that the servant raise his hands and He return them empty, with no good in them. So when one of you raises his hands, let him say: O Living, O Sustainer, there is no god but You, O Most Merciful of the merciful, three times. Then when he lowers his hands, let him pour the good upon his face.""**"
"17341","Salmān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of people raising their palms to Allah, Glory be to Him, asking Him for something, and of its becoming due upon Allah to place in their hands what they asked. > **""No people raise their palms to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, asking Him for something, except that it becomes due upon Allah to place in their hands what they asked.""**"
"17342","Khālid ibn al-Walīd رضي الله عنه reported that he complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of straitness in his dwelling, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to lift his hands heavenward and ask Allah for spaciousness. > **""Raise your hands to the sky and ask Allah for spaciousness.""**"
"17343","Khallād ibn al-Sāʾib reported from his father رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he supplicated, would raise his palms toward his face."
"17344","Jarīr رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing at ʿArafa with his cloak tucked under his arm, raising his hands no higher than his head while his sinews trembled."
"17345","Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaḥyā reported that he saw ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما observe a man raising his hands supplicating before finishing his prayer, and after the man finished, Ibn al-Zubayr told him of the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding raising the hands only after completing prayer. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not raise his hands until he had finished his prayer.""**"
"17346","Abū Bakra رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded asking Allah with the bellies of the palms and forbade asking with their backs. > **""Ask Allah with the bellies of your palms, and do not ask Him with their backs.""**"
"17347","Abū Hubayra reported from Ḥabīb ibn Maslama al-Fihrī رضي الله عنه, who was one whose supplication was answered, that when placed in command of an army and meeting the enemy, he told the people what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a company whose supplication Allah answers, then praised Allah, asked protection of blood and reward of martyrs, whereupon the enemy commander, called al-hanbāṭ, descended and entered his tent. > **""No company gathers where one of them supplicates and the rest say āmīn, except that Allah answers them.""**"
"17348","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that when a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for something, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he was unable to be like the old woman of Banū Isrāʾīl, and when the Companions asked her story, he told of Mūsā عليه السلام losing the way when commanded to march with Banū Isrāʾīl, the scholars explaining Yūsuf's عليه السلام covenant that his bones be carried from Egypt, none knowing the grave but the old woman, her refusal to guide except for her stipulated reward of being with him in Paradise, its weighing heavy until granted, her leading them to a pond, draining it, digging, and the road shining like daylight once the bones were lifted; in the second wording a Bedouin honoured by the Prophet ﷺ asked only for a she-camel and goats, prompting the same lesson, with the same account of the covenant, the refusal, the revelation to grant her stipulation, the draining and digging, and the road appearing like daylight. > **""Were you unable to be like the old woman of Banū Isrāʾīl?""**"
"17349","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when asked and intending to grant, would say yes, and when intending otherwise would remain silent, never saying no for anything; a Bedouin asked repeatedly until the Prophet ﷺ, as though reproving, told him to ask whatever he wished, whereupon those present hoped he would ask Paradise, but he asked for a mount and then for provisions, and the Prophet ﷺ granted each; then the Prophet ﷺ marvelled at the distance between his asking and that of the old woman of Banū Isrāʾīl, and told of Mūsā عليه السلام reaching the sea with turned-back mounts, being told he stood at the grave of Yūsuf عليه السلام levelled with the earth, seeking the old woman who knew it, her oath-bound refusal except for companionship in his rank of Paradise, his urging her to ask merely Paradise, her oath-bound refusal of anything but being with him, and the revelation commanding the grant as no loss, after which she guided, the bones were removed, and the sea was crossed. > **""Ask whatever you wish, O Bedouin. How far between the asking of the Bedouin and the old woman of Banū Isrāʾīl!""**"
"17350","Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriya رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded that when asking Allah, al-Firdaws be asked, for it is the crown of Paradise, just as a man tells his shepherd to keep to the crown of the valley for its richer pasture. > **""When you ask Allah, ask Him for al-Firdaws, for it is the crown of Paradise.""**"
"17351","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that no servant seeks refuge from the Fire seven times but the Fire asks Allah to shelter him, and none asks Paradise seven times but Paradise asks Allah to lodge him therein, or words to that effect. > **""No servant seeks refuge from the Fire seven times except that the Fire says: O Allah, shelter him from me; and none asks Paradise seven times except that Paradise says: O Allah, lodge him in me.""**"
"17352","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked whether they loved to strive in supplication and instructed them to say a supplication for help in remembrance, gratitude, and excellent worship. > **""O Allah, help us in Your remembrance, Your gratitude, and excellent worship of You.""**"
"17353","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported supplications heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which he would never abandon while alive, concerning magnifying thanks, abundant remembrance, following counsel, and guarding behest. > **""O Allah, make me magnify Your thanks, increase Your remembrance, follow Your counsel, and guard Your behest.""**"
"17354","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication seeking forgiveness for what preceded and followed, what was secret and open, excess, and what Allah knows better, affirming Him Advancer and Delayer with no god but Him. > **""O Allah, forgive me what I advanced and what I delayed, what I concealed and what I disclosed, my excess, and what You know better than I; You are the Advancer and You are the Delayer; there is no god but You.""**"
"17355","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication asking help in remembrance, gratitude, and excellent worship. > **""O Allah, help me in Your remembrance, Your gratitude, and excellent worship of You.""**"
"17356","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the general supplication of the Prophet ﷺ sought forgiveness for error and deliberation, secrecy and openness, ignorance and intention. > **""O Allah, forgive me what I erred in and what I intended, what I concealed and what I disclosed, what I did in ignorance and what I intended.""**"
"17357","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate for forgiveness of sins, wrongdoing, jest, earnestness, intention, and all such present with them. > **""O Allah, forgive us our sins, our wrongdoing, our jest, our earnestness, our intention, and all of that which is with us.""**"
"17358","ʿAwn ibn ʿAbdillāh reported that he met an old man in Syria, asked whether he had heard anything from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the man affirmed hearing him say a brief supplication for forgiveness and mercy. > **""O Allah, forgive us and have mercy upon us.""**"
"17359","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ offered to teach what Jibrīl عليه السلام had taught him, and upon acceptance instructed a supplication for forgiveness of error, intention, jest, and earnestness, for the blessing of gifts not to be withheld and trial in withheld things to be averted. > **""O Allah, forgive me my error and my intention, my jest and my earnestness; do not deprive me of the blessing of what You granted me, and do not try me in what You withheld from me.""**"
"17360","Abū Isḥāq reported that al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه offered to teach a supplication taught to him by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to be said when people rival over gold and silver, asking firmness in command, resolve of guidance, thanks for blessing, patience in trial, excellent worship, contentment with decree, sound heart, truthful tongue, the good of His knowledge, refuge from its evil, and forgiveness for what He knows. > **""O Allah, I ask You for firmness in the affair, resolve in guidance, thanks for Your blessing, patience in Your trial, excellent worship of You, contentment with Your decree; I ask You for a sound heart, a truthful tongue; I ask You from the good of what You know, seek refuge in You from the evil of what You know, and seek Your forgiveness for what You know.""**"
"17361","Awsaṭ Abū ʿAmr al-Bajalī reported that coming to Medina a year after the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he found Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه addressing the people, telling that the Messenger ﷺ had stood among them the previous year, choked by weeping three times, then commanded asking Allah for well-being, since none was granted like certainty after well-being, nor anything severer than doubt after disbelief. > **""O people, ask Allah for well-being, for none was granted like certainty after well-being, and nothing is severer than doubt after disbelief.""**"
"17362","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that a youth came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking to be taught a supplication attaining good; the Prophet ﷺ drew him near until his knee nearly touched the Prophet's knee, then taught a supplication of pardon to the Pardoning Lover of pardon, Generous Pardoner. > **""O Allah, pardon me, for You are Pardoning, You love pardon, and You are Pardoning, Generous.""**"
"17363","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking that as his form was beautified, so his character be made excellent. > **""O Allah, as You beautified my form, make excellent my character.""**"
"17364","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking that as his form was beautified, so his character be made excellent. > **""O Allah, as You beautified my form, make excellent my character.""**"
"17365","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he never prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ without hearing him say upon finishing a supplication for forgiveness of error and intention and guidance to sound deeds and manners which none guides to nor averts their evil but Allah. > **""O Allah, forgive me my error and my intention. O Allah, guide me to sound deeds and manners, for none guides to their soundness nor averts their evil but You.""**"
"17366","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported that he never prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ without hearing him say upon leaving prayer a supplication for forgiveness of error and all sins, revival, provision, and mending toward sound deeds and manners which none guides to nor averts their evil but Allah. > **""O Allah, forgive me my error and all my sins. O Allah, revive me, provide for me, and mend me toward sound deeds and manners; none guides to their soundness nor averts their evil but You.""**"
"17367","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for health, chastity, trustworthiness, good character, and contentment with decree; in the other wording, protection in place of health. > **""O Allah, I ask You for health, chastity, trustworthiness, good character, and contentment with decree.""**"
"17368","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever says the covenant supplication to the Lord of heavens and earth, Knower of unseen and seen, testifying sole worship and Muḥammad His servant and Messenger, confessing entrustment to the self nears evil and distances good, trusting only mercy, and asking a covenant fulfilled on Resurrection Day, Allah will command His angels to fulfil His servant's covenant and admit him to Paradise; Suhayl reported informing al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who replied that every veiled girl among his household says it in her chamber. > **""O Allah, Lord of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the seen, I covenant to You in this worldly life that I testify there is no god but You alone without partner, and that Muḥammad is Your servant and Messenger; if You entrust me to myself You bring me near evil and distance me from good, and I trust only in Your mercy, so make for me with You a covenant You fulfil to me on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You do not break the promise.""**"
"17369","Abū Hurayra رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bequeathed to Salmān al-Khayr رضي الله عنه words intended as a gift, to be asked of the Most Merciful and supplicated with night and day, concerning soundness of faith, faith in good character, success followed by prosperity, mercy, well-being, forgiveness, and pleasure. > **""O Allah, I ask You for soundness of faith, faith with good character, success followed by prosperity, mercy from You, well-being, forgiveness from You, and pleasure.""**"
"17370","The delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays reported that they heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say a supplication to be made among the chosen servants, the bright-foreheaded, the accepted delegation; when they asked each meaning, he explained the righteous servants, those whose places of purification whiten, and a delegation of this nation proceeding with their Prophet to their Lord, Blessed and Exalted. > **""O Allah, make us among Your chosen servants, the bright-foreheaded, the accepted delegation.""**"
"17371","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a brief supplication asking forgiveness, mercy, and guidance to the straightest path. > **""My Lord, forgive, have mercy, and guide me to the straightest path.""**"
"17372","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him over half Yemen and Muʿādh over half, Abū Mūsā came greeting him, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to say a supplication for guidance and straightness, recalling with guidance its direction and with straightening the straightening of his arrow. > **""O Allah, guide me and make me straight.""**"
"17373","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that servants never asked anything better than that He forgive them and grant them well-being. > **""Servants never asked anything better than that He forgive them and grant them well-being.""**"
"17374","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his uncle al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه to supplicate often for well-being. > **""O uncle, supplicate often for well-being.""**"
"17375","Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضi الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to teach something to ask Allah; the Prophet ﷺ told him to ask his Lord for well-being; after some days he returned asking again, and the Prophet ﷺ addressed him as uncle of the Messenger and instructed asking Allah for well-being in this world and the Hereafter. > **""O ʿAbbās, O uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ask Allah for well-being in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"17376","In another narration the questioner said he supplicates with something from morning till night, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed him to ask Allah for well-being. > **""Ask Allah for well-being.""**"
"17377","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no supplication of a servant is more beloved to Allah than saying a supplication asking pardon and well-being in this world and the Hereafter. > **""O Allah, I ask You for pardon and well-being in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"17378","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar reported that he was with ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar رضي الله عنهما when a man asked for beneficial supplications; Ibn Jaʿfar recalled hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answer the same question with a command to ask pardon and well-being in this world and the Hereafter. > **""Ask Allah for pardon and well-being in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"17379","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking pardon and well-being in religion, world, family, and wealth, covering of faults, safety from fear, guarding from front, behind, right, left, and above, with refuge from being seized from beneath. > **""O Allah, I ask You for pardon and well-being in my religion, my world, my family, and my wealth. O Allah, cover my faults, secure my fears, guard me from before me, behind me, my right, my left, and above me; I seek refuge in You, O Allah, from being seized from beneath me.""**"
"17380","Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet رضي الله عنها, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he used to supplicate with long comprehensive words, beginning with the First before whom nothing was and the Last after whom nothing is, refuge from every controlled creature, sin, laziness, grave punishment, trials of wealth and poverty, and sin and debt, cleansing like the white garment and distance like east from west, then this is what Muḥammad asked his Lord, followed by requests for the best supplication, asking, success, deed, reward, life, death, firmness, heavy scales, raised rank, accepted prayer, forgiven fault, highest ranks of Paradise, raising of remembrance, lightening of burden, rectitude, purity, and deliverance from Fire, each sealed with āmīn. > **""O Allah, You are the First, nothing before You; You are the Last, nothing after You. I seek refuge in You from the evil of every creature whose forelock is in Your Hand. I ask You for the best supplication, the best asking, the best success, the best deed, the best reward, the best life, the best death. Make me firm, weight my scales, raise my rank, accept my prayer, forgive my fault, and I ask You for the highest ranks of Paradise. O Allah, deliver me from the Fire.""**"
"17381","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ often said in supplication a plea to the Turner of hearts for firmness upon His religion; when she asked whether hearts turn, he affirmed every son of Adam's heart lies between two fingers of Allah, established or deviated as He wills, and taught asking that hearts not deviate after guidance with mercy from Him, Most Generous; then when she asked for a supplication for herself, he taught refuge as Lord of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ with forgiveness, removal of heart's rancour, and protection from misleading trials while alive. > **""O Allah, Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion. O Allah, Lord of the Prophet Muḥammad, forgive my sin, remove the rancour of my heart, and shield me from misleading trials as long as You keep us alive.""**"
"17382","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported in raised form that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a plea to the Turner of hearts for firmness; when asked whether fear remained despite belief in his message, he affirmed concerning hearts, with al-Aʿmash signalling with two fingers. > **""O Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion.""**"
"17383","Anas رضi الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say in supplication a plea to turn his heart toward His religion and to guard with mercy those behind them. > **""O Allah, turn my heart toward Your religion, and guard those behind us with Your mercy.""**"
"17384","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a plea to the Guardian of Islam and its people for firmness therein until meeting Him. > **""O Guardian of Islam and its people, keep me firm therein until I meet You.""**"
"17385","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he used to supplicate with similar longer words, beginning with the First and Last, refuge from controlled creatures, sin, laziness, Fire and grave punishment, trials of wealth and poverty, sin and debt, cleansing of the heart like the white garment, distance like east from west, then this is what Muḥammad asked his Lord, followed by best asking and supplication, firmness, weighty scales, affirmed faith, raised rank, accepted prayer, forgiven fault, highest Paradise, deliverance from Fire, night and day forgiveness, righteous abode, safe salvation from Fire, secure entry to Paradise, blessing in self, character, form, family, life, hearing, sight, spirit, and death, and weighty good deeds. > **""O Allah, You are the First, nothing before You; You are the Last, nothing after You. I ask You for the best asking, the best supplication, the best success, the best deed, the best reward, the best life, the best death. Make me firm, weight my scales, affirm my faith, raise my rank, accept my prayer, forgive my fault, and I ask You for the highest ranks of Paradise. O Allah, deliver me from the Fire and admit me to Paradise secure.""**"
"17386","Umm al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنها reported that Faḍāla ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه used to say a supplication asking contentment with decree, cool life after death, delight of gazing at His Face, and longing to meet Him without harmful affliction or misleading trial, claiming they were supplications the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say. > **""O Allah, I ask You for contentment with decree, cool life after death, delight of gazing at Your Face, and longing to meet You, without harmful affliction or misleading trial.""**"
"17387","Al-Sāʾib al-Thaqafī reported that he was with ʿAmmār رضي الله عنه while he supplicated in prayer; a man came, and ʿAmmār instructed him with a supplication by unseen knowledge and power over creation for life while good and seizure when death is better, awe in unseen and seen, truth in pleasure and anger, moderation in wealth and poverty, contentment with decree, cool life after death, longing to meet without harmful affliction or misleading trial, adornment of faith, and guidance; then offered better words as though raised to the Prophet ﷺ, to be said upon taking night rest, of surrendered soul, directed face, entrusted affair, belief in the revealed Book and sent Prophet, the soul created with life and death to Him, mercy if taken and guarding in faith if delayed. > **""O Allah, by Your knowledge of the unseen and Your power over creation, keep me alive while You know life is better for me, and seize me when You know death is better for me. O Allah, I surrendered my soul to You, directed my face to You, entrusted my affair to You; I believed in Your revealed Book and Your sent Prophet. My soul is a soul You created; to You its life and to You its death; if You take it, have mercy on it, and if You delay it, guard it with the guarding of faith.""**"
"17388","ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ رضi الله عنه and a woman of Qays, or in the other version Quraysh, reported that each heard the Prophet ﷺ, one hearing a supplication for forgiveness of sin, error, and intention, the other hearing a supplication seeking guidance to the soundest affair and refuge from the evil of the soul. > **""O Allah, forgive me my sin, my error, and my intention. O Allah, I seek Your guidance to the soundest of my affair, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of my soul.""**"
"17389","An old woman of Banū Numayr reported that she watched the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying at al-Abṭaḥ facing the House before the Hijra and heard him say a supplication for forgiveness of sin, error, and ignorance. > **""O Allah, forgive me my sin, my error, and my ignorance.""**"
"17390","Busr ibn Arṭāh al-Qurashī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicate for a good end in all affairs and shelter from disgrace of this world and punishment of the Hereafter, with the addition that whoever keeps it as supplication dies before trial befalls him. > **""O Allah, make good our end in all affairs, and shelter us from the disgrace of this world and the punishment of the Hereafter.""**"
"17391","Abū Ṣirma رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for his own enrichment and that of his freedman. > **""O Allah, I ask You for my enrichment and the enrichment of my freedman.""**"
"17392","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate for enjoyment of hearing and sight made inherited from him, well-being in religion, resurrection upon the life granted, help against oppressors with witnessed retribution, surrendered religion, directed face, entrusted affair, supported back, no refuge nor escape but to Him, and belief in His sent Messenger and revealed Book. > **""O Allah, grant me enjoyment of my hearing and my sight until You make them inherited from me; grant me well-being in my religion, resurrect me upon what You kept me alive, help me against whoever wronged me until You show me my retribution from him. O Allah, I surrendered my religion to You, turned my face to You, entrusted my affair to You, leaned my back upon You; no refuge nor escape from You except to You. I believed in Your Messenger whom You sent and Your Book which You revealed.""**"
"17393","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ often supplicated to be made fearful as though ever seeing Him until meeting Him, felicitous in taqwā, not wretched in disobedience, choosing good in decree, blessing in measure so as neither to hasten delay nor delay hastening, enrichment in soul, enjoyment of hearing and sight made inherited, help against oppressors with witnessed retribution and cooled eye. > **""O Allah, make me fear You as though I ever see You until I meet You; make me felicitous in Your taqwā, do not make me wretched in disobeying You; choose good for me in Your decree, bless me in Your measure so I love neither hastening what You delayed nor delaying what You hastened; place my enrichment in my soul, grant me enjoyment of my hearing and my sight and make them inherited from me; help me against whoever wronged me, show me my retribution in him, and cool my eye thereby.""**"
"17394","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضi الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication for enjoyment of hearing and sight made inherited, help against oppressors, and witnessed retribution. > **""O Allah, grant me enjoyment of my hearing and my sight, make them inherited from me, help me against whoever wronged me, and show me my retribution from him.""**"
"17395","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr رضi الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication for enjoyment of hearing and sight made inherited from him. > **""O Allah, grant me enjoyment of my hearing and my sight, and make them inherited from me.""**"
"17396","Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه reported that Jibrīl عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ, declaring none sent to was dearer to him, and offered to teach names among Allah's names most beloved to Him to be supplicated with, concerning light, adornment, compeller, support, originator, sustainer of heavens and earth, majesty, succour of criers, refuge of seekers, goal of worshippers, reliever of distressed, comforter of grieved, answerer of compelled, remover of hardship, God of worlds, Most Merciful, before whom every need is laid. > **""O Light of the heavens and the earth, O Adornment of the heavens and the earth, O Compeller of the heavens and the earth, O Support of the heavens and the earth, O Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Sustainer of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of majesty and honour, O Succour of those crying for help, O Refuge of seekers of refuge and Goal of worshippers, Reliever of the distressed, Comforter of the grieved, Answerer of the compelled, Remover of hardship, O God of the worlds, O Most Merciful of the merciful; every need is laid before You.""**"
"17397","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to teach them words asking rectitude between them, union of hearts, guidance to paths of peace, deliverance from darkness to light, avoidance of shameful deeds open and hidden, blessing in hearing, sight, hearts, spirits, offspring, repentance as Most Relenting Merciful, gratitude, praise, acceptance of blessings, and their completion. > **""O Allah, rectify what is between us, unite our hearts, guide us to the paths of peace, deliver us from darkness to light, keep us from shameful deeds, open and hidden. O Allah, bless for us our hearing, our sight, our hearts, our spirits, and our offspring; turn to us, indeed You are the Relenting, the Merciful; make us thankful for Your blessings, praising them, accepting them, and complete them for us.""**"
"17398","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for a pure life, a sound death, and a return neither disgracing nor exposing. > **""O Allah, I ask You for a pure life, a sound death, and a return neither disgracing nor exposing.""**"
"17399","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met him and offered to teach words taught to whomever Allah intends good for, and upon acceptance instructed a supplication of weakness strengthened in His pleasure, lowliness honoured, and poverty enriched. > **""O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen my weakness in Your pleasure; I am lowly, so honour me; I am poor, so enrich me.""**"
"17400","Ṣuhayb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate affirming Allah is no newly made deity nor contrived lord, with none before Him to resort to besides Him, nor helper in creation to associate with Him, Blessed and Exalted; Kaʿb added that thus used to supplicate Dāwud the Prophet of Allah عليه السلام. > **""O Allah, You are no deity we newly made, nor a lord we contrived, nor was there for us before You a god to resort to and leave You, nor did anyone help You in creating us that we should associate him with You. Blessed are You and Exalted.""**"
"17401","Abū Hurayra رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, supplicated and sought refuge with words the people had never heard the like of; when asked how they might supplicate and seek refuge likewise, he instructed asking by what Muḥammad His servant and Messenger asked, and refuge by what His servant and Messenger sought refuge from. > **""O Allah, we ask You by what Muḥammad Your servant and Messenger asked You, and we seek refuge in what Your servant and Messenger sought refuge.""**"
"17402","Abū Umāma رضi الله عنه reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he supplicated at length beyond memorising; then he offered to inform of something gathering it all, asking from what Muḥammad his Prophet and Messenger asked and refuge from what he sought refuge from, with help sought from Him, conveyance upon Him, and no power but by Allah. > **""O Allah, we ask You from what Muḥammad Your Prophet and Messenger asked You, and we seek refuge in You from what Your Prophet Muḥammad Your servant and Messenger sought refuge from. You are the One whose help is sought, upon You is conveyance, and there is no power nor strength except by Allah.""**"
"17403","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, shall I not teach you a supplication by which even sins numbering particles would be forgiven, though already forgiven for you, addressing the Forbearing Generous with glorification and blessing as Lord of the mighty Throne. > **""O Allah, there is no god but You, the Forbearing, the Generous; glory be to You, blessed are You, Lord of the mighty Throne.""**"
"17404","Khabbāb al-Khuzāʿī رضi الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say a supplication asking covering of faults, securing of fear, and settlement of debt. > **""O Allah, cover my fault, secure my fear, and settle my debt for me.""**"
"17405","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضi الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say it suffices a man supplicating to say a brief supplication for forgiveness, mercy, and entry to Paradise. > **""O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, and admit me to Paradise.""**"
"17406","Abū Umāma رضi الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded saying a supplication asking for a soul tranquil in Him, believing in meeting Him, content with His decree and His grant. > **""O Allah, I ask You for a soul tranquil in You, believing in meeting You, content with Your decree, and satisfied with Your grant.""**"
"17407","Maymūna bint Abī ʿUsayb, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah رضي الله عنها, reported that a woman of Jurash came on a camel calling, O ʿĀʾisha, help me with a supplication of the Messenger ﷺ to settle or assure me; ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها told her to place her right hand upon her belly, wipe it, and say the healing supplication in Allah's Name for cure, healing, enrichment beyond others, and removal of harm; Rabīʿa said she supplicated with it and found it good, while al-Muntajaʿ thought Rabīʿa meant the woman was other than herself. > **""In the Name of Allah. O Allah, treat me with Your remedy, heal me with Your healing, enrich me by Your grace from any besides You, and ward Your harm from me.""**"
"17408","Anas ibn Mālik رضi الله عنه reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque until sunrise, when he went out followed by Anas to enter upon Fāṭima bint Muḥammad رضي الله عنها, found her lying asleep, asked what kept her sleeping at that hour, she complained of fever since last night, he asked after the supplication taught to her, she said she forgot it, and he instructed the Ever-Living Sustainer plea for mercy-aided rectitude of all affairs and non-entrustment to self or people for a blink. > **""O Ever-Living, O Sustainer, by Your mercy I seek aid; rectify for me all my affairs, and do not entrust me to myself for the blink of an eye, nor to anyone among people.""**"
"17409","Ibn ʿUmar رضi الله عنهما reported that among the supplication of the Prophet ﷺ was a plea not to be entrusted to himself for a blink nor to be stripped of sound gifts granted. > **""O Allah, do not entrust me to myself for the blink of an eye, and do not strip from me the sound of what You granted me.""**"
"17410","Ibn ʿUmar رضi الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate with words, the narrator thinking he said, asking for faith touching the heart until knowing only the decreed befalls, and contentment in livelihood with the apportionment. > **""O Allah, I ask You for faith touching my heart until I know that nothing befalls me except what You decreed for me, and contentment in livelihood with what You apportioned for me.""**"
"17411","Al-Zubayr رضi الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication asking blessing in religion which guards affairs, in the Hereafter which is the destination, in the world which is conveyance, with life made increase in every good and death made rest from every evil. > **""O Allah, bless for me my religion which guards my affair, my Hereafter which is my destination, and my world wherein is my conveyance; make my life increase in every good, and make death rest for me from every evil.""**"
"17412","Burayda رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking to be made grateful, patient, small in his own eyes and great in people's eyes. > **""O Allah, make me grateful, make me patient, make me small in my own eyes and great in people's eyes.""**"
"17413","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, or another, reported that Ḥuṣayn came to the Prophet ﷺ reproaching that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib fed liver and hump while he slaughtered his people; the Prophet ﷺ answered as Allah willed, and when asked what to say, instructed a supplication shielding from the soul's evil and resolving to the soundest affair; the man departed, embraced Islam, returned asking what to say now, and was instructed a supplication forgiving secret, open, error, intention, known, and unknown. > **""O Allah, shield me from the evil of my soul, and resolve for me to the soundest of my affair. O Allah, forgive me what I concealed and what I disclosed, what I erred in and what I intended, what I knew and what I was ignorant of.""**"
"17414","Thawbān رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for pure things, abandoning denials, love of the poor, repentance upon him, and seizure untested if trial is intended for servants. > **""O Allah, I ask You for pure things, abandoning denials, love of the poor, and that You turn to me; and if You intend trial for Your servants, seize me untested.""**"
"17415","Makḥūl reported that he entered upon Anas ibn Mālik رضi الله عنه and heard him mention that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate for benefit in teaching and teaching of benefit. > **""O Allah, benefit me by what You taught me, and teach me what benefits me.""**"
"17416","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for beneficial knowledge and seeking refuge from knowledge that does not benefit. > **""O Allah, I ask You for beneficial knowledge, and I seek refuge in You from knowledge that does not benefit.""**"
"17417","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication for beneficial knowledge and refuge from unbeneficial knowledge, in his own person. > **""O Allah, I ask You for beneficial knowledge, and I seek refuge in You from knowledge that does not benefit.""**"
"17418","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying a supplication for beneficial knowledge and an accepted deed. > **""O Allah, I ask You for beneficial knowledge and an accepted deed.""**"
"17419","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say in his supplication a phrase asking for a protection like the protection of the newborn child. > **""A protection like the protection of the newborn.""**"
"17420","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say a supplication asking for the most ample provision at old age and at the ending of life. > **""O Allah, make the most ample of Your provision upon me at my old age and at the ending of my life.""**"
"17421","Al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar reported that he entered upon ʿAlī رضي الله عنه after ʿishāʾ, declared his love for him, and ʿAlī offered to teach him a supplication taught to him by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about opening the heart and granting obedience. > **""O Allah, open the hearings of my heart to Your remembrance, and grant me obedience to You, obedience to Your Messenger ﷺ, and action by Your Book.""**"
"17422","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade saying an unrestricted request for prompting of proof, since even the disbeliever meets his proof, and instructed instead to ask for the proof of faith at death. > **""O Allah, prompt me the proof of faith at death.""**"
"17423","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه to Abyssinia, he accompanied him and provisioned him with words asking for gentleness in every difficulty and for ease and well-being in this world and the Hereafter. > **""O Allah, be gentle with me in easing every difficulty, for easing every difficulty upon You is easy, and I ask You for ease and well-being in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"17424","Abū Burayda al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, O Burayda, shall I not teach you words which whomever Allah intends good for He teaches him, then he never forgets them, concerning weakness, forelock, contentment, strength, honour, and enrichment. > **""O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen my weakness in Your pleasure, take my forelock toward good, make Islam the utmost of my contentment. O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen me; I am lowly, so honour me; I am poor, so enrich me.""**"
"17425","Samura رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate asking that blessing, adornment, and tranquillity be placed in their land. > **""O Allah, place in our land its blessing, its adornment, and its tranquillity.""**"
"17426","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that when Allah sent Adam عليه السلام down to the earth, he stood, came to the Kaʿba, prayed two rakʿas, and Allah inspired him with a supplication of confession of need, forgiveness, penetrating faith, true certainty, and contentment; then Allah revealed to him acceptance of repentance and promise of forgiveness, sufficiency, repelling of Satan, trade beyond traders, and the coming of the world humbled, for whoever supplicates with it. > **""O Allah, You know my secret and my open, so accept my excuse; You know my need, so grant me my request; You know what is in my soul, so forgive my sin. O Allah, I ask You for faith that touches my heart, true certainty until I know that nothing befalls me except what You decreed for me, and contentment with what You apportioned for me.""**"
"17427","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to teach the words spoken by Mūsā عليه السلام when he crossed the sea with Banū Isrāʾīl, and they accepted; ʿAbdullāh never abandoned them since hearing them from the Messenger ﷺ, Shaqīq never abandoned them since hearing them from ʿAbdullāh, al-Aʿmash never abandoned them since hearing them from Shaqīq, and a visitor in a dream told al-Aʿmash, O Sulaymān, to add a request for help against inner corruption and rectitude of all affairs. > **""O Allah, to You is praise, to You is complaint, You are the One whose help is sought, and there is no power nor strength except by Allah the Most High the Most Great.""**"
"17428","Ṣuhayb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate affirming that Allah is no newly invented deity nor contrived lord, with no god before Him to resort to, and no helper in creation to associate with Him; Kaʿb added that thus used to supplicate Dāwud the Prophet of Allah عليه السلام. > **""O Allah, You are no deity we newly made, nor a lord we contrived, nor was there for us before You a god to resort to and leave You, nor did anyone help You in creating us that we should associate him with You. Blessed are You and Exalted.""**"
"17429","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that among the supplication of Dāwud the Prophet عليه السلام was refuge from wealth that becomes trial, offspring that becomes woe, the evil mirror that brings grey hair before old age, and the evil neighbour whose eyes watch and ears listen, burying good and spreading evil; and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that an evil neighbour in the abode of residence breaks the back. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from wealth that is a trial upon me, from offspring that is a woe upon me, from the evil mirror that brings grey hair before old age, and I seek refuge in You from an evil neighbour whose eyes watch me and whose ears listen to me; if he sees good he buries it, and if he sees evil he spreads it.""**"
"17430","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that when they supplicated they used to say, asking for the prayer of a righteous people upon them, neither sinful leaders nor wicked, who stand by night and fast by day. > **""O Allah, place upon us the prayer of a righteous people, neither leaders of sin nor wicked, who stand the night and fast the day.""**"
"17431","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sabra reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, when he entered morning, used to say a supplication asking to be among the greatest of His servants in share of every morning-apportioned good, guiding light, spread mercy, extended provision, removed harm, lifted trial, and averted fitna. > **""O Allah, make me among the greatest of Your servants in share of every good You apportion in the morning, light that guides, mercy You spread, provision You extend, harm You remove, trial You lift, and fitna You turn away.""**"
"17432","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr reported that Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما used to say a supplication asking by the light of His Face by which heavens and earth shine, to be placed in His refuge, protection, neighbourhood, and beneath His shelter. > **""O Allah, I ask You by the light of Your Face by which the heavens and the earth shine, to place me in Your refuge, Your protection, Your neighbourhood, and beneath Your shelter.""**"
"17433","ʿUrwa ibn Ruwaym reported from al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriya رضي الله عنه, an aged Companion who loved to be taken in death and used to supplicate for seizure on account of old age and frail bones; while in the mosque of Damascus a handsome youth in green asked what he supplicated, instructed him to say a better wording of good deed and reached term, and when asked who he was, said he was Rībail who removes sorrow from believers' hearts. > **""O Allah, the deed is good and the term has reached.""**"
"17434","Al-Aswad ibn Yazīd reported that ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه recited, Except him who has taken with the Most Merciful a covenant, and explained that Allah will say on Resurrection Day that whoever has a covenant with Him should stand; when asked to teach, he instructed a covenant supplication of the Originator of heavens and earth, Knower of unseen and seen, entrusting reliance and requesting fulfilment on Resurrection Day, with Zakarīya adding through al-Qāsim the state of fear, refuge, repentance, and desire. > **""O Allah, Originator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the seen, I covenant to You in this worldly life that if You entrust me to myself You bring me near evil and distance me from good, and I trust only in Your mercy, so make it for me with You a covenant You fulfil to me on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You do not break the promise.""**"
"17435","Abū al-Aḥwaṣ reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, supplicate asking by the ample blessing bestowed, the trial by which tested, and the grace favoured, to be admitted to Paradise by grace, favour, and mercy. > **""O Allah, I ask You by Your ample blessing with which You blessed me, by Your trial with which You tried me, and by Your grace with which You favoured me, to admit me to Paradise. O Allah, admit me to Paradise by Your grace, Your favour, and Your mercy.""**"
"17436","Abū Qilāba reported from Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that he used to say a supplication asking, if written among the wretched, to be erased and affirmed among the felicitous. > **""O Allah, if You have written me among the people of wretchedness, then erase me and affirm me among the people of felicity.""**"
"17437","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUkaym reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to supplicate for increase in faith, certainty, and understanding, or he said knowledge. > **""O Allah, increase me in faith, certainty, and understanding.""**"
"17438","Thawr ibn Yazīd reported that Muʿādh رضي الله عنه, when he rose for tahajjud by night, used to say a supplication addressing the Ever-Living Sustainer while eyes slept and stars set, confessing slow seeking of Paradise and weak flight from Fire, and asking for a guidance kept with Him to be returned on Resurrection Day. > **""O Allah, eyes have slept, stars have set, and You are Ever-Living, Sustainer. O Allah, my seeking of Paradise is slow and my flight from the Fire is weak. O Allah, make for me with You a guidance You return to me on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You do not break the promise.""**"
"17439","ʿAbdullāh ibn Qurt reported that he was lagging upon his camel while with Khālid ibn al-Walīd رضي الله عنه, intended to abandon it, then supplicated to Allah, so Allah raised it for him and he mounted."
"17440","Burayda رضي الله عنه reported that while the Prophet ﷺ was on a journey, they came upon a man tossing in the midday heat saying to his soul, sleep by night, falsehood by day, yet hoping for Paradise; when he finished disciplining himself he came to them, the Prophet ﷺ said, take your brother, they asked him to supplicate for them, he asked union upon guidance, provision of taqwā, then at the Prophet's instruction for more, success, then Paradise as their return. > **""O Allah, gather their affair upon guidance. O Allah, make taqwā their provision. O Allah, grant him success. O Allah, make Paradise their return.""**"
"17441","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ missed him on Friday, came to him after prayer and asked why unseen; he explained detention by a Jew owed an ounce of gold; the Prophet ﷺ offered a supplication by which even debt like Ṣabr, a mountain in Yemen, would be paid, instructing the long praise of the Owner of sovereignty, merger of night and day, bringer of living and dead, boundless provider, Most Merciful of world and Hereafter, and request for enriching mercy. > **""O Allah, Owner of sovereignty, You grant sovereignty to whom You will and strip sovereignty from whom You will, honour whom You will and abase whom You will; in Your Hand is good; indeed You are over all things Powerful. You merge night into day and merge day into night, bring forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living, and provide whom You will without account. Most Merciful of this world and the Hereafter and Most Merciful to them, You grant from them to whom You will and withhold from whom You will; have mercy upon me with a mercy that enriches me from the mercy of any besides You.""**"
"17442","Muʿādh رضي الله عنه reported in another narration that a man had a claim upon him so he stayed home two days from shame, then came to the Messenger ﷺ who asked what kept him, and offered words by which even mountain-like debts would be settled; he instructed similar wording in brief, with the addition of enrichment from poverty, settlement of debt, and death in worship and jihād in His path. > **""O Allah, enrich me from poverty, settle my debt for me, and take me in Your worship and jihād in Your path.""**"
"17443","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Muʿādh رضي الله عنه, shall I not teach you a supplication by which even debt like Mount Uhud would be paid for you, instructing a shorter form of the Owner of sovereignty with boundless provision and enriching mercy. > **""O Allah, Owner of sovereignty, You grant sovereignty to whom You will and strip sovereignty from whom You will, honour whom You will and abase whom You will; in Your Hand is good; indeed You are over all things Powerful. Most Merciful of this world and the Hereafter, You grant them to whom You will and withhold from them whom You will; have mercy upon me with a mercy that enriches me from the mercy of any besides You.""**"
"17444","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that her father رضي الله عنه offered to teach her a supplication taught to him by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said ʿĪsā عليه السلام used to teach it to the disciples, by which even debt like Uhud would be settled, addressing the Reliever of anxiety, Remover of distress, Answerer of the compelled, Most Merciful of world and God of Hereafter. > **""O Allah, Reliever of anxiety, Remover of distress, Answerer of the supplication of the compelled, Most Merciful of this world and God of the Hereafter, You are my Merciful; have mercy upon me with a mercy that enriches me from any besides You.""**"
"17445","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no one afflicted by anxiety or sorrow who says the supplication of servitude, forelock in His Hand, just decree, asking by every Name named, revealed, taught, or reserved in unseen knowledge, to make the Quran the spring of his heart, light of chest, polish of sorrow, and removal of anxiety, except Allah removes his anxiety and replaces sorrow with relief; they asked whether to learn them, and he said yes, whoever hears them should learn them. > **""O Allah, I am Your servant, son of Your servant, son of Your maidservant; my forelock is in Your Hand, Your decree over me is executed, Your judgment over me is just. I ask You by every Name belonging to You, by which You named Yourself, sent down in Your Book, taught to any of Your creation, or reserved in the knowledge of the unseen with You, to make the Quran the spring of my heart, the light of my chest, the polish of my sorrow, and the removal of my anxiety.""**"
"17446","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that when one of you fears a ruler, let him name the feared one and seek neighbourhood with the Lord of seven heavens and mighty Throne against him, jinn, men, and followers exceeding against him. > **""O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and Lord of the mighty Throne, be for me a protector against the evil of so-and-so, son of so-and-so, and the evil of jinn and men and their followers, lest any of them exceed against me. Mighty is Your protection, exalted is Your praise, and there is no god besides You.""**"
"17447","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said that when you come to an awe-inspiring ruler fearing his assault, say exaltation above all creation and refuge with the Withholder of seven heavens from falling except by permission, against His servant so-and-so, his armies, followers, and partisans of jinn and men. > **""Allah is Greatest, Allah is greater than all His creation, Allah is mightier than what I fear and dread. I seek refuge in Allah who withholds the seven heavens from falling upon the earth except by His permission, from the evil of Your servant so-and-so, his armies, followers, and partisans of jinn and men. My God, be for me a protector against their evil. Exalted is Your praise, mighty is Your protection, blessed is Your Name, and there is no god besides You.""**"
"17448","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضi الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach them istikhāra, asking good by His knowledge, ability by His power, and grace and mercy in His Hand, confessing knowledge and power belong to Him, Knower of unseen, and asking facilitation of the intended matter if good in religion, world, and outcome, or facilitation to good wherever it is. > **""O Allah, I seek good from You by Your knowledge, seek ability from You by Your power, and ask You from Your grace and mercy, for they are in Your Hand none owns besides You; for You know and I do not know, You decree and I do not decree, and You are Knower of the unseen. O Allah, if this matter You intend for me is good for me in my religion and my world and the outcome of my affair, then grant me success in it and ease it; and if other than it is good, then grant me success in good wherever it is.""**"
"17449","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ was sitting among people when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn رضي الله عنهما passed, so he said, bring my two sons that I may seek refuge for them as Ibrāhīm عليه السلام sought refuge for his sons Ismāʿīl and Isḥāq, with the perfect words against devil, pest, and envious eye. > **""I seek refuge for you two by the perfect words of Allah from every devil and pest, and from every envious eye.""**"
"17450","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that with a refuge Ibrāhīm عليه السلام used to seek refuge for Isḥāq and Ismāʿīl, he seeks refuge for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn رضي الله عنهما, concerning Allah hearing the supplicant beyond Whom is the aim of whoever casts. > **""Allah heard the supplicant for whoever supplicated; beyond Allah is the aim for whoever casts.""**"
"17451","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say refuge from deafness and dumbness, sin and debt, drowning, and anxiety. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from deafness and dumbness, I seek refuge in You from sin and debt, I seek refuge in You from drowning, and I seek refuge in You from anxiety.""**"
"17452","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say refuge from incapacity, laziness, old age, cowardice, and miserliness. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from incapacity, laziness, old age, cowardice, and miserliness.""**"
"17453","Quṭba رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ seeking refuge from evil matters and vain desires. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from evil matters and vain desires.""**"
"17454","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say refuge from Satan, his pricking, blowing, and spittle, and from grave punishment; when asked, he explained pricking as whispering, spittle as poetry, blowing as doubts cast in prayer to cut it off, and said most grave punishment is from urine. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Satan, from his pricking, his blowing, and his spittle, and from the punishment of the grave.""**"
